WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.700
So I've started reading this amazing
book by Richard Powers.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.980
And in his book he says the best arguments in the world

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.260
won't change a person mind.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.740
The only thing that can do that is a good story.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:11.940
I kind of agree with that.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:15.060
Because I've been working on climate change

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.780
since 02004 and this entire time

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.480
I've been talking to lots of different people.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:22.720
And it's really hard just to show data and show facts

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.380
like the the slides that I started my talk off with today.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.960
That usually doesn't change people's minds.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.860
Especially if  their minds are already made up.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.660
So I think stories are really important.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.420
Neil Gaiman actually gave a talk at the Long Now.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.480
And he basically says all good stories change you.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.340
And I think that's really powerful.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:42.660
All good stories do change us.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.760
But sometimes we don't know how they changed us.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.920
And sometimes the change takes place like

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.580
way way way into the future.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:52.740
So I'm trying to figure out how to deal with that.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.600
So what type of action am I trying to
get at?

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.540
We need a price on carbon.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.680
I think all of us will agree with that.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.740
We really need a price on carbon right now.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.780
And so we need public support for a price on carbon.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.200
And Washington state is starting to do that.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.140
We need to change individual behavior as well

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:14.700
that reflects our new transition pathway that we're trying to get to.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.100
And so a lot of my past work has looked

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.220
at facts and feelings separately.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:20.660
I've sort of kept them separate and I've been

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.680
toggling between both of them.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.200
Because looking at them together makes things

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.500
really really messy. So what I've looked at is

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.960
how do people understand what is most effective

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.580
for them to change in their own lives.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.220
So if I were to ask you today,

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.240
what is the single most effective thing you can do

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.040
to decrease your energy consumption:

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.160
would you know?

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.300
Do you want to shout out some answers?

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:48.100
'Don't have kids' 'Don''t eat meat'

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.689
'Don't fly' 'Don't fly'

00:01:50.689 --> 00:01:54.400
'Electric cars' 'Food waste'

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.880
All right so we have a really good list!

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.560
I'm going to show you what the modal response is

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.600
in the United States. You're gonna be really surprised.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.600
And then I've also been looking at

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.120
how do you change social norms?

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.120
How do you get people to pay attention to this problem?

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.020
Because attention is actually a very limited resource.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.980
So think of attention is like a very small pond of water

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.060
that you carry with you everywhere you go.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:18.000
And every time someone asks for something

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.580
you're like giving them a little bit of that water.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.460
And then you're like 'Oh my god where did my water go?'

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:22.580
I need to go and back into my cave.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.320
So that's what attention is...

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.980
it's a seriously limited resource.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.240
So I asked you this question: in your opinion,

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:32.900
what is the most effective thing?

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.960
Here are the responses that we get.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.660
Since the 1980s this has been the #1 modal response

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.920
that people give to this open-ended question

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.860
"what is the single most effective thing?"

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:47.560
The modal response, starting with Willett Kempton

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.340
in the 1980s, has been "turning off the lights."

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.420
And this is when you ask people very broadly,

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.900
hey, what is the single most effective thing?

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.460
Very similar to what I asked you.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:59.760
However a surprising finding from my work

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.860
that I'm not going to show you,
but I'll talk to you about it for

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.620
a second, is if you ask people

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:06.100
'what is the single most effective thing 
other Americans can do?'

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:07.440
Do you know what the answers are?

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.040
All of the ones that you told me.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.480
So 'Carpooling' 'Getting an efficient car'

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.420
'Getting an electric car' 'Biking instead of driving'...

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:19.260
So the answers really change.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.720
It's not that people don't know what is effective.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:25.670
It's that they don't want to do those behaviors themselves.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.360
I'm telling you more challenges than solutions.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.200
So turning off the lights is great please do that.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.620
But it's not the most effective thing.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:39.440
You guys know already.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.060
So... given my training in physics and in engineering

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:46.060
I was really interested in how do people

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.340
quantitatively estimate how much 
energy different appliances use.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:51.600
If I were to ask you do you know what the difference is

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.940
between a watt and a watt-hour, do you know?

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.920
You guys know. Okay... two hands went up.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:59.160
Do you guys know? Yeah, three hands went up.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:02.260
Okay, alright... a couple of guys in the back..

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:03.760
and there's a friend saying yeah he knows...

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.680
Okay they're all pointing at each other.
I don't think they really know.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:10.140
So a lot of people do not know

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.100
what a watt and a watt-hour is, right?

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.400
So they don't know what the units

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:14.240
of energy are.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.120
So what I wanted to understand is,

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.360
if I were to ask people about different appliances

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:21.360
in their homes how well do they understand

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.860
how much energy different appliances use.
Just broadly.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:26.640
So what I first had to do--and over here on the y-axis

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.080
you have "perceived energy used or saved" 
in watt-hours.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:32.480
On the x-axis you have "actual energy used or saved"
in watt-hours.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.220
This is a log-log scale so it goes from 1, 10, 100...

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.420
so on and so forth.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.680
So data that lies along the diagonal line

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.640
means people's perceptions match reality. Okay?

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:46.200
So what I said was: imagine a 100 watt light bulb.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:51.700
A 100 watt light bulb being left on for an hour
uses "100 units" of energy.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:53.760
Now how many units of energy
would your dishwasher use?

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.960
Would you guys be able to... kind of?

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.800
Yeah in the same amount of time in one hour.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.880
So that was the type of question I asked people.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.220
I won't put you on the spot. I'll just show you the data.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:12.060
So I told everyone a 100 watt light bulb 
uses a hundred units of energy.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.460
How many units of energy would
these different appliances use?

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.080
So in  general the orange line is the overall curve.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.640
But I'm gonna walk you through it with some examples.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:24.020
So laptops are overestimated by a factor of two.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:26.300
So people think it's equivalent to one light bulb

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:27.700
when it's actually equivalent to half.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:30.340
And this is data from 02009, so now

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:33.120
laptops are becoming way way way more efficient.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.340
Changing the washer setting from hot to cold

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:41.100
actually is underestimated by a factor of 40.

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.280
So people think changing from hot to cold

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:45.240
is equivalent to saving one light bulb when it's

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:47.700
actually equivalent to saving 40 lightbulbs.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:52.600
So this is the entire set of appliances that we looked at.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:56.240
And so what you notice is that 
when you start using a lot of energy

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:57.940
when you start thinking about appliances

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:01.140
that use a lot of energy there's a huge underestimation.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:03.740
A lot of this data is below the diagonal line.

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:05.180
Not just that.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:08.220
People are not able to correctly differentiate

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:11.120
between these appliances.
So there's this compression bias.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:13.040
So what we really need to do

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:15.080
if we are thinking about energy use is we need

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:17.620
to take that curve and pull it up towards the diagonal.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:20.020
How do we make people accurate?

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:21.420
Then there's the other question,

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:23.080
which I hope you guys will ask me:

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:26.300
Does accuracy matter if we have limited effort?

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.720
And I would say, yeah, it does. If people think that

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.200
some of these ineffective behaviors are really effective,

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:32.880
and they're not that's really problematic.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:37.000
So... does anyone have accurate perceptions?

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:39.879
So, this is disclaimer #2,

00:06:39.879 --> 00:06:43.060
we asked 2 electrical engineers. 
And they're really accurate.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:48.960
So engineers, yeah, we're awesome.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.580
So engineers do really well on this task.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:54.340
And so what we did was

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:56.700
we tested a manufactured heuristic.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:59.080
Let me break that down and what that means.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.780
A heuristic is basically a rule, right?

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:05.500
So I can give you a rule to enact a particular behavior.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:08.540
A manufactured heuristic is a manufactured rule

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:10.700
that I give you to use when you're making a decision.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:13.160
So if I were to tell you large appliances

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:15.800
that heat and cool use a lot more energy than you think.

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:17.320
That actually is a heuristic

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:19.780
that if you're making your energy estimates

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:22.260
that actually pulls that curve up towards the diagonal.

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:25.260
So I'm not teaching you what the difference is between

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:27.320
watts and watt-hours. I'm not

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:28.780
telling you how to compute

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:30.360
how much energy different appliances use.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.020
I'm just giving you a very simple rule.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:35.580
So that's what the heuristic condition is right here.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:38.940
So you can see the baseline basically
replicates the original data.

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:41.560
The heuristic condition bumps the slope up.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:43.520
And then if I give you multiple anchors,

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.280
so I just gave one light bulb in the first condition.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:48.700
But if I were to give you an LED and

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:50.640
I were to give you a dishwasher,

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:54.300
that actually helps you gearshift
between these different appliances.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:57.240
So that actually improves your perceptions a lot.

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:59.360
So there are ways that we found that

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:01.920
actually are able to correct people's misperceptions

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:04.119
of energy use. Sort of circumventing

00:08:04.119 --> 00:08:06.220
teaching people basic energy physics.

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:09.500
You guys with me so far?
Yeah? Okay, great.

